=== environmental incident: disk pressure and OOM-killed backends (found and resolved before trusting the regression) ===
df -h / showed the host at 99% capacity (2.9GB free of 234GB), dominated by large pre-existing Docker images unrelated to this project (open-webui, plane-*, opensearch, debezium, neo4j, kindest/node). docker builder prune -f and docker image prune -f both reclaimed 0B (no dangling/reclaimable content under default pruning) -- this session is not authorized to remove other projects' images.

docker ps -a --filter name=ocor-bootstrap showed:
ocor-bootstrap-fuseki-1        Exited (137) 7 hours ago
ocor-bootstrap-spire-agent-1   Exited (1) 5 hours ago

Both OOM-killed/exited independently of this task's own code. Restarted both as routine, reversible maintenance on the project's own bootstrap stack (docker start ocor-bootstrap-fuseki-1; docker start ocor-bootstrap-spire-agent-1) -- confirmed fuseki healthy via docker inspect --format '{{.State.Health.Status}}'. Never touched eci-dev-control-plane or open-webui.

Considered and reverted an in-flight tmpfs redesign of the sealed backbone.py (git checkout -- ocor-runtime/src/ocor_runtime/c5/backbone.py) after recognizing it -- and test_ocor_dev_0040.py -- are hash-referenced sealed deliverables of already-merged OCOR-DEV-0040; the real root cause was the OOM-killed backends above, not backbone.py's own disk footprint, so no source change to the sealed deliverable was needed.

